## A Janus cobalt nanoparticles and molybdenum carbide decorated
Keywords: Co nanoparticles, $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}$ , heterostructures, N-doped carbon, overall water splitting.
## 1. Introduction
Sustainable hydrogen energy has been considered as a promising and effective alternative to fossil fuels owing to the increasing global environmental concerns and energy shortage [1–3]. Among advanced energy technologies, the environmentally friendly and feasible electrochemical water electrolysis has drawn extensive attention to produce high-purity hydrogen fuel [4]. Water splitting contains two half-cell reactions including cathodic hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and anodic oxygen evolution reaction (OER) [5,6]. However, both the HER and OER possess high dynamic overpotential owing to the huge energy barriers for adsorption/desorption of intermediate species and a low electron transfer rate, which is the major bottleneck of the water electrolysis technology [7,8]. The design of electrocatalysts is essential for lowering the energy barrier and dynamic overpotential. Recently, the state-of-the-art electrocatalysts for OER are noble metal oxide $(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ), while Platinum $(\mathrm{Pt})$ -based compounds exhibit efficient HER performance [9,10]. Unfortunately, the high cost, earth scarcity and inferior chemical stability hinder their practical application [11]. Thus, alternative low-cost electrocatalysts with superior performance and excellent stability are important and necessary for water splitting.
In recent years, molybdenum carbide $(\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C})$ based electrocatalysts have demonstrated impressive HER performance, which possess a similar $d.$ -band electronic structure with Pt-like materials and show a remarkable hydrogen-adsorption ability [12,13]. Molybdenum (Mo) contains unoccupied $d$ -bands, thus boosting the electrocatalytic HER process of $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ materials due to the electron-rich property [14]. The $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ suffers from a strong adsorption of $\mathrm{H}$ , which is advantageous to the reduction of $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ , but not conducive to the desorption of hydrogen molecule [15]. Recently, some studies found that increasing the number of active sites by regulating the electron density is a possible route to obtain enhanced HER performance for $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ [16]. Meanwhile, $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}$ -based materials possess unsatisfactory OER performance, which has become the key obstacle for commercial application of overall water splitting electrolyzers [17,18]. It is very urgent to design bifunctional electrocatalysts with efficient HER and OER performance. Fortunately, some reports found that Co-based catalysts show excellent OER activity, approaching comparable to that of noble metal oxide $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}\left[19{-}21\right]$ . In general, the Co-based materials have an inferior HER performance [22,23]. Thus, constructing the $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ based or Co-contained heterostructures may be an effective strategy to achieve simultaneous HER and OER in a single nanostructure.
Recently, heteroatoms (such as N, S, and P) doped carbon nanostructure have unique electronic structures and show an enhanced electrocatalysis performance, attracting great attention of researchers [24–27]. N-doped carbon nanomaterials coupled with transition metal nanoparticles have shown excellent activity for OER and HER due to the construction of more active sites [28]. Besides, N doping into carbon framework will regulate the electronic state of the carbon materials, boost the electrons transfer, optimize the adsorption/desorption process and intermediate species and products, and enhance the electrocatalysis performance [29]. Ouyang et al. also revealed $\mathrm{Co}$ and $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ nanoparticles decorated N-doped carbon nanotubes hybrid materials can act as a bifunctional material for water splitting by experimental results and density functional theory (DFT) calculations [30]. The reported results indicated that constructing the Co and $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ heterostructures can reduce the overpotential of OER process at the Co sites. Encapsulated $\mathsf{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ optimized reactants-chemisorption strength via the coupled Mo-N bond. Furthermore, Li and co-workers have constructed the Ni and $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ embedded with N-doped carbon nanofibers for high-performance water splitting [31]. Those previous results illustrate that the design of bifunctional active sites can achieve exceptional electrocatalysis activity.
Inspired by above understanding, we design a general method for crafting Janus heterostructures possessing superior HER $(\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C})$ and OER (Co) decorated N-Doped carbon $(\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC-800})$ from N-enriched metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) as bifunctional electrocatalysts for simultaneous HER, OER and overall water splitting. The facile and low-cost heterostructures show excellent HER and OER activity with a low overpotential and small Tafel slope. Furthermore, the hybrid material also exhibits a superior long-time operation stability for overall water splitting in alkaline medium. This work might offer a promising thought to fabricate bifunctional hybrids structure for overall water splitting.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials and chemicals
All the regents are directly used as purchased without further purification. 4,5-dicyanoimidazole (DCI, $\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ , $99\%$ ) was obtained from Aladdin Chemistry Co. Ltd. N,N-dimethylformamide $(\mathrm{DMF},\ge99.5\%)$ , ammonium molybdate tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{(NH_{4})_{6}M o_{7}O_{28}{\cdot}{4H_{2}O},\geq99\%}$ ) and cobalt acetate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}$ , $\ge98\%$ ) were received from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ (20 wt%) was supplied by Alfa Aesar (China) Chemicals Co., Ltd. Iridium dioxide $(\mathrm{Ir}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , $99.9\%$ ) was afforded by Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. Nafion $10\ \mathrm{\wt\%}$ was provided by Sigma-Aldrich. The carbon nanotubes (CNT, $>95\%$ ) was supplied by Chengdu Organic Chemicals Co. Ltd.
## 2.2. Material preparation
## 2.2.1. The synthesis of MOFs precursor (DCI-Co)
The DCI-Co was synthesized according to the literature [32].
## 2.2.2. The synthesis of CoNC
The DCI-Co was heated at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $120~\mathrm{min}$ , then the temperature was raised to $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and annealed for $120~\mathrm{min}$ under Ar atmosphere with a ramp rate of $5~{^\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ to obtain CoNC.
## 2.2.3. The synthesis of CoNC-800
The CoNC was heated at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ , then the temperature was raised to $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and pyrolyzed for $120~\mathrm{{min}}$ under Ar atmosphere with a ramp rate of $5~^{\circ}C$ $\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ to obtain CoNC-800.
2.2.4. The synthesis of $\mathrm{C}\mathbf{0}/\mathrm{M}\mathbf{0}_{2}\mathrm{C}\mathbf{\mathcal{O}}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}8\mathbf{0}\mathbf{0}$
## 2.2.5. The synthesis of CoNC-800 (Acid treatment)
For comparison, the CoNC-800 was dispersed in $8\mathrm{~M~HNO}_{3}$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ under stirring to remove a part of Co nanoparticles. The CoNC-800 (Acid treatment) was collect by cleaning with distilled water and drying in oven.
## 2.3. Material characterization
The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was performed by Tecnai G2 F30 S-TWIN. Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra were conducted on Nicolet iS50 Getting Started FT-IR spectrophotometer. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were reflected by Shimadzu XRD-6000 X-ray diffractometer. The X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra were investigated by MultiLab 2000. The surface area of electrocatalysts were reflected by TriStar II 3020 Surface Area and Porosity Analyzer.
## 2.4. Electrocatalytic Study
Electrochemical experiments were conducted by electrochemical workstation (CHI760E) with a three-electrode configuration. The graphite rod acted as counter electrode, and $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ electrode (KCl saturated) was used as reference electrode. The electrocatalysts coated carbon paper ${\mathrm{1~cm}}\times{\mathrm{1~cm}}{\mathrm{)}}$ acted as working electrode. 4 mg of prepared electrocatalysts ( $2~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ or $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) and $15~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ Nafion was added into $985~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of 2-propanol/deionized water $\mathrm{(v/v}=1{:}3)$ solution under sonication. 500 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of electrocatalysts ink were drop-casted on carbon paper. The HER and OER performance were evaluated in $1\ \mathrm{M\KOH}$ solution by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) with a scan rate of $5\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . The potentials were referred to reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) by $\mathrm{E}(\nu s.\mathrm{\RHE})=\mathrm{E}(\nu s.\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl})+0.197+0.059\mathrm{pH}$ . The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was conducted within the frequency range of 100000 to $0.1\ \mathrm{Hz}$ at an AC amplitude of $5~\mathrm{mV}$ . All the electrochemical experiments were performed without $i R$ compensation.
## 3. Results and discussion
The bifunctional heterostructures were constructed as Scheme 1 (details shown in experimental section). As shown in Fig. 1a, the crystal structure of DCI-Co precursor was examined by XRD, the DCI-Co possess sharp diffraction peaks, suggesting a good crystallinity. These peaks match with the typical structure of zeolitic imidazole frameworks, and the similar result has been reported in previous literature [33]. The FT-IR spectroscopy was further used to investigate to confirm the structure of MOFs precursor. The characteristic peaks located at $1350{-}1500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $900{-}1350\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ are the stretching and plane vibrations for imidazole ring of both DCI and DCI-Co, and the peaks below $800~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ can be ascribed to out-of-plane vibration for imidazole ring [34]. The vibration peaks at 2247 and $3107~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be indexed to cyano groups in DCI. The new bands at $1608~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in DCI-Co indicates the construction of $\mathrm{Co-N_{x}}$ bonds (Fig. 1b) [32]. The XRD and FT-IR spectroscopy results illustrate that the DCI-Co precursor have been fabricated successfully. After a carbonation process, the CoNC nanocarbon precursor was obtained. As displayed in Fig. 2, the SEM and TEM images show that CoNC hierarchical nanocarbons possess some carbon nanotubes (CNTs) and the metal nanoparticles are encapsulated in bamboo-like CNTs and carbon matrix. The XRD measurements indicate that the diffraction peaks of metal nanoparticles in the CoNC hierarchical structure are distinguished with (111), (200) and (220) crystal planes of zerovalent state Co (JCPDS No. 15-0806, Fig. S1) [35].
As shown in Figs. S2a, S2b, 3a and 3b, the TEM images exhibit that both CoNC-800 and $\mathrm{Co/Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{\cdot}800{-}2$ with a hierarchical structure composed by CNTs and carbon matrix. The HR-TEM results confirm that the lattice spacing of metal nanoparticles in CoNC-800 is $0.20\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which can be attributed to the (111) planes of cubic Co (Figs. S2c and S2d) [36]. After the construction of heterostructures, there are two kinds of lattice spacings for encapsulated metal nanoparticles in HR-TEM images, the lattice spacings of 0.23 and $0.20\ \mathrm{nm}$ can be indexed to the (211) plane of $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ and the (111) plane of Co (Figs. 3c–e) [37], respectively. The high-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM, Figs. 4a and S3a) and TEM elemental mapping images (Figs. 4b–f and Figs. S3b–f) express homogeneously distributed C, N, O, Co and Mo in obtained hierarchical heterostructures. Besides, the EDS elemental analysis further confirm the existence of C, N, O, Co and Mo (Fig. S4). Those results show that the successful introduction of Mo source. As shown in Fig. 5a, the Co nanoparticles exist in CoNC-800 (JCPDS No. 15-0806), which is similar to CoNC. The diffraction peaks of Mo contained heterostructures can be indexed well to cubic Co (JCPDS No. 15-0806) and orthorhombic $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ (JCPDS No. 77-0720) (Figs. 5b and S5) [38,39]. HR-TEM and XRD results can well explain that the Co nanoparticles and $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ decorated N-Doped carbon have been successfully prepared.
Fig. 6 are the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherm of as-synthesized electrocatalysts. All the materials exhibit type IV isotherm, indicating the presence of mesopores [40]. The corresponding pore size distributions further verify that pores in materials are mainly mesopores (inside of Fig. 6). The BET surface areas of CoNC-800, Co/Mo2C@NC-800-1, $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}\mathscr{@}\mathrm{NC}{-}800{-}2$ and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{\cdot}800{-}3$ are 363.94, 145.97, 15.78 and $6.02\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the surface chemical states of CoNC-800 and $\mathrm{Co/Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{-}800{-}2$ were explored by XPS. As revealed in Fig. S6a, the CoNC-800 possess C, N, O and Co. After the formation of Co nanoparticles and $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ heterostructures, there is a clear peak for Mo in $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C@N C-800-2}$ (Fig. S7). The C 1s spectra of CoNC-800 and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}\mathscr{@}\mathrm{NC}-800-2$ reveal four types peak of $C{=}C$ , C=N&C-O, C-N& $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ and O- $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ , respectively (Figs. 7a and S6b) [41]. The deconvolution of N 1s spectrum of CoNC-800 can be matched into pyridinic-N, pyrrolic-N and graphitic-N (Fig. S6c) [42]. After the fabrication of heterostructures hybrid material, the N 1s spectrum of Co/Mo2C@NC-800-2 can be divided into four Mo-N, pyridinic-N, pyrrolic-N and graphitic-N (Fig. 7b) [43]. In addition, the Co 2p XPS spectrum of CoNC-800 and Co/Mo2C@NC-800-2 can be assigned to six components, indicating the presence of zero-valence state Co (Figs. S6d and 7c) [44, 45]. There are some $C{\bf0}^{2+}$ in those samples, which is the Co exposure in air and that is unavoidable to be oxidized. Fig. 7d is Mo 3d XPS spectrum, binding can be attributed to C-bound Mo in $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ and O-bound Mo [46, 47]. The reason of the existence of O-bound Mo is similar to $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ formation mechanism. The XPS results further reveal that the heterostructure electrocatalysts have been successfully prepared.
The HER performances of electrocatalysts were investigated on a three-electrode setup by LSV curves in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH solution. As shown in Fig. 8a, the LSV curves illustrate that the $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}\mathrm{-}800{-}2$ possesses the lowest overpotential of $121~\mathrm{mV}$ at current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ $\gamma_{I I O}=121\ \mathrm{mV}$ , which is smaller than that of CoNC-800 $\begin{array}{c c c}{{(\eta_{I O}}}&{{=}}&{{135\mathrm{~\mV)}}}\end{array}$ , $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C@N C-800-1}$ $\begin{array}{c c c}{{(\eta_{I O}}}&{{=}}&{{123\quad\mathrm{mV})}}\end{array}$ and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}\mathscr{@}\mathrm{NC}\mathrm{-}800{-3}$ $\gamma_{I0}=146~\mathrm{mV}$ ). Besides, the $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}\mathrm{-}800{-}2$ also shows a lower overpotential than that of CoNC-800, $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C@N C-800-1}$ and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{-}800{-}3$ at $50\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ (Table S1). In Fig. 8b, $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}(\mathcal{Q})$ NC-800-2 achieves a current density of 43.58 and $86.41\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at the overpotential of $250\mathrm{mV}$ and $350\ \mathrm{mV}\ (j_{250}=43.58,j_{350}=86.41\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2})}$ , respectively, which is larger than that of CoNC-800 $(j_{250}=31.32,j_{350}=58.27\mathrm{~mA~cm^{-2}})$ ), $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C(\it{\overline{{{\omega}}}N C-800-1}\mathrm{\Omega}(j_{250}=38.51\mathrm{\Omega}}$ , $j_{350}=76.25\mathrm{~mA~cm^{-2}})$ and $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C(\overline{{{\omega}}}N C-800-3\ (j_{250}=33.56,\it j_{350}=69.34\ m A\ c m^{-2})}$ . The Tafel plots is a type indicator to investigate the HER kinetics and a smaller Tafel slope represents a better electrocatalytic kinetics [48]. Obviously, $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}\mathrm{-}800{-}2$ exhibits the lowest Tafel slope of $166.85~\mathrm{mV}$ dec−1 (Fig. 8c), which is significantly outperforming that of CoNC-800 (226.30 mV dec−1), Co/Mo2C@NC-800-1 ( $215.77~\mathrm{\mV~dec^{-1}}$ ) and Co/Mo2C@NC-800-3 (218.06 mV dec−1). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy was measured to deeper research the kinetic of the electrocatalysis process. As shown in Fig. S8, $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}_{\mathscr{O}}\mathrm{\Gamma}_{}$ NC-800-2 has the smallest charge transfer resistance, indicating a fastest electron transfer for HER [49]. All the above results show that the crafting heterostructures will greatly boost the HER performance of electrocatalysts. Compared to previous reported molybdenum or Co-containing materials, $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}\mathrm{-}800{-}2$ also displays a prominent activity, such as $\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{Mo}_{2}@\mathrm{NC}$ (η10 = 218 mV) [44] and $\mathrm{Co_{2}P/M o_{2}C/M o_{3}C o_{3}C(\overline{{{\omega}}}C\textit{\xi}(\eta_{I0}=182\ m V)}$ [50] (Table S2). The stability is of importance to evaluate whether a catalyst possesses a practical application prospect. The chronoamperometry (i-t) curve shows a superior durability for $\mathrm{Co/Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{-}800{-}2$ after a long-time operation of $20\mathrm{~h~}$ and maintain this current density of $91.9\%$ compared to initial state (Fig. 8d). In contrast, the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ exhibit inferior stability, suggesting a potential commercial value for $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C@N C-800-2}$ .
The OER polarization curves are shown in Fig. 9a: the $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C@N C-800-2}$ shows the lowest overpotential of 311 and $415~\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve the current density of 10 and $50\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ ( $\eta_{I O}=311~\mathrm{mV}$ , $\eta_{50}=415~\mathrm{mV}$ (Table S3), respectively, which is remarkably lower than that of CoNC-800 $(\eta_{I O}~=~375~\mathrm{\mV}_{;}$ $\eta_{50}~=~499~\mathrm{{\mV})}$ , $\mathrm{Co/Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC-800-1}$ ( $\gamma_{I0}=313~\mathrm{mV}$ , $\eta_{50}=430\mathrm{mV}$ ) and $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C(\overline{{\omega}}N C-800-3\ (\eta_{10}=}$ $339\mathrm{mV}$ , $\eta_{50}=450\ \mathrm{mV}$ ). $\mathrm{Co/Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{\cdot}800{-}2$ reveals the highest current density of
21.84 and $67.73\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with the overpotential of 350 and $450~\mathrm{mV}$ $(j_{350}=21.84\mathrm{mA}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2},j_{450}=67.73\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2},$ ) than that of CoNC-800 $(j_{350}=6.38\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $j_{450}=30.04$ mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ), $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C(\overline{{\omega}}N C-800-1~(j_{350}=19.77~m A~c m^{-2},j_{450}=58.67~m A~c m^{-3})}$ and $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C\textcircled{\omega}N C-800-3}~(j_{3\thinspace50}~=~12.73~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm^{-2}},~j_{4\thinspace5\theta}~=~50.49~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm^{-2}})$ (Fig. 9b), respectively. The improved catalytic kinetics of the heterostructures is revealed by Tafel plots, the Tafel slope of CoNC-800, $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C@N C-800-1}_{\cdot}$ $\mathrm{Co/Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}\mathrm{-}800{-}2$ and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{-}800{-}3$ are $145.54~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-}}$ 1, $142.78\ \mathrm{mV}$ dec−1, 131.50 mV dec−1 and 143.89 mV dec−1 (Fig. 9c), respectively. Electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) of the materials were estimated by double-layer capacitance $\mathrm{(C_{dl})}$ via CV with different scan rates to deeper understand the boosting OER activity (Fig. S9). The $\mathrm{Co/Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{-}800{-}2$ exhibits the highest $\mathrm{C_{dl}}$ value of $319.82~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ , which is much larger than that of CoNC-800 (75.07 mF $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ), $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C@N C-800-1}$ $274.07\ensuremath{\mathrm{~mF~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{cm}}^{-2})$ and Co/Mo2C@NC-800-3 (170.49 mF $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ) (Fig. 9d), suggesting that the increased number of active sites after the construction of heterostructures [51]. To further investigate the OER kinetics, EIS was measured and shown in Fig. 9e. $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}\mathrm{-}800{-}2$ shows the smallest charge resistance, indicating a fastest interfacial electronic transfer property [52]. Thus, the crafting heterostructure is favorable for constructing more active sites and enhancing the charge transfer kinetics. The Co/Mo2C@NC-800-2 suppresses many advanced molybdenum or cobalt-containing electrocatalysts in alkaline electrolyte, such as $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{CS}$ (M2) [18] and Co-Mo2C@NCNT [47] (Table S4). The $\mathrm{Co/Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{\cdot}800{-}2$ also displays an much improved stability than that of noble metal-containing $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ after $20\mathrm{h}$ OER test.
Due to the outstanding activity for both HER and OER, the $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C@N C-800-2}$ has been utilized as both cathode and anode $\left(\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C\varpi N C-800-2\Lambda\Omega\left(+\right)\Lambda\ U\ C o/M o_{2}C\varpi N C-800-2\Lambda\left(-\right)}\right)$ for an electrolyzer with a two-electrode cell. As shown in Fig. 10a, the $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C@N C-800-2}$ based electrolyzer shows a cell voltage of 1.67 and $1.75{\mathrm{~V~}}$ to achieve water-splitting current density of 10 and $20\mathrm{mAcm^{-2}}(E_{I O}=1.67\mathrm{V},E_{20}=1.75\mathrm{V})$ , surpassing CoNC-800 $\mathrm{\Delta}E_{I O}=1.77\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ , $E_{20}~=~1.88~\mathrm{~V})$ , $\mathrm{Co/Mo_{2}C@N C-800-1}$ $(E_{I O}~=~1.70~\mathrm{~V~}$ , $E_{20}~=~1.79~\mathrm{~V~}$ and $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{-}800{-}3$ $\mathit{\Delta}E_{I O}=1.70\mathrm{~V~}_{!}$ , $E_{20}=1.83\mathrm{~V}_{.}$ (Table S5). The crafting hybrid material shows a more positive potential than that of previously reported electrocatalysts $10~\mathrm{\mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , such as $\mathrm{Co}_{0.85}\mathrm{Se}@\mathrm{NC}$ $(E_{I0}~=~1.76~\mathrm{V})$ [53] and NC/Co/CoP/CP $\mathrm{\Delta}E_{I0}=1.72\mathrm{\DeltaV})$ [54] (Table S6). Besides, excellent long-time overall water splitting operation of $30\mathrm{~h~}$ is achieved for $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{\cdot}800{-}2$ , while the commercial state-of-the-art noble metal-based materials reveal very poor stability (Fig. 10b). Those results illustrate that the $\mathrm{Co}$ and $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ heterostructures material is a superior bifunctional electrocatalyst for overall water splitting.
The combination of Co nanoparticles and $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ heterostructures are beneficial for increasing active sites and boosting the sluggish kinetics. The $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}$ , Co coupled with N-doped carbon will achieve a lower adsorption free energy of reactants for HER, and reduce the overpotential for Co sites, thus accelerating OER process [30]. Based on above experimental results, the superior electrocatalytic performance of crafting hybrid material can be ascribed to the synergistic effect of Co, $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ and coupled hierarchical N-doped carbon.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, unlike randomly mixed MOFs’ precursors to obtain alloys or agglomerated metal nanostructures [61,62]. We develop a facile and general strategy for crafting of Co and homogeneously distributed $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ bifunctional heterostructures decorated N-doped carbon $(\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C@NC}{-}800{-}2)$ as high-performance and durable electrocatalysts for overall water splitting. The construction of heterostructures can effectively reduce overpotential and facilitate the kinetics for hybrid material during HER, OER and overall water splitting process. The $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}\mathscr{@}\mathrm{NC}-800-2$ achieve the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at the overpotentials of 121 and $311~\mathrm{mV}$ for HER and OER on carbon paper in 1.0 M KOH solution, surpassing numerous previously reported Co or Mo-containing bifunctional electrocatalysts [18,50,63]. The heterostructures decorated N-doped carbon serves as both cathode and anode for overall water splitting, which exhibits a small cell voltage of $1.67\mathrm{V}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and achieves a robust long-time operation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ evolution. Furthermore, a series of experiment results also illustrate that the doped N, wrapped Co and $\mathrm{Mo}_{2}\mathrm{C}$ are of importance to promote the electrocatalysis performance. This work is promising for designing high-efficiency and low-cost electrocatalysts for water splitting devices to achieve large-scale hydrogen generation in the future.
